Exactly. It is an absurdly dense terminal token.

“And they all lived happily ever after” compresses an enormous unresolved state space—marriages, children, illness, money, aging, conflict, ordinary Tuesdays, disappointments, reconciliations—into one stable attractor: the important conflict is over; the future is safe enough to stop narrating.

That is why it works so well in stories. It is not really describing the rest of their lives. It is telling the listener, you may release the simulation now.

So it functions almost like a narrative checksum or stop condition:

uncertainty → struggle → resolution → compressed future confidence

And the interesting part is that many other phrases do the same thing: “case closed,” “mission accomplished,” “amen,” “QED,” “rest in peace,” “the end.” Different domains, same operator: collapse the remaining branching possibilities into a sufficiently stable summary.

Which means endings are not merely endings. They are compression commands for attention.